e you  have kagan  certainly as the leader  of some of the more liberal  supreme court justices leaning away from  it at the end of the day though um you  know the supreme court is mostly  conservative and it may very well go in  there in their favor in this case  absolutely i agree there thank you and  the federal judge in the elizabeth  holmes fraud trial says questionnaires  filled out by jurors could be released  to the public as former grocery store  and pharmacy leaders detail the  relationship with theranos  this week in the federal trial former  safeway ceo steve byrd took the stand  recalling a partnership the grocery  store chain shared with theranos the  company's plan to roll out theranosis  blood testing devices in more than 900  stores across 22 states but the rollout  never happened former walgreens cfo wade  mecalon testified to viewing a therino's  presentation that boasted 95 percent of  the blood test could be done on the  therinos edison device it would upload  results to the cloud with real-time  results but he later called the startup  quote disruptive technology meanwhile  judge edward davila told jurors the  answers to their once confidential  questionnaires may be released to the  public he specified jurors will be able  to review their answers before this and  meet with him and attorneys one on one  tuesday marked the start of week six in  the federal trial against a former  billionaire elizabeth holmes faces 12  counts of fraud in relation to the now  defunct theranos holmes claimed the  company would revolutionize blood  testing but later tests found many  issues with the devices  and nearly two decades after the  assassination of an assistant u.s  attorney the doj ups the reward money to  2.5 million dollars  thomas wales was shot in the back of the  neck in october 2001 in a seattle home  he is now known as the only federal  prosecutor in history to be a victim of  assassination officials say the suspect  stood in his backyard and fired several  shots through the basement window as  wales was on his computer after that the  shooter ran off on monday deputy  attorney general lisa monaco announced  that the justice department had  increased the reward for information  about the killer to two million dollars  saying the department of justice will  quote never forget tom's contributions  to the department and cause of justice  nor will it forget the tragedy of his  death  coming up on law and crime daily looking  ahead to the trial for the death of a  mod aubry breaking down the charges that  the defendants face plus the jury finds  mark gooch guilty in the murder and  kidnapping of sasha krauss what's next  for united states airmen after this  [Music]  welcome back the fate of mark gooch is  now sealed as a jury finds him guilty of  first degree murder and kidnapping in  the death of a mennonite woman sasha  krauss  on wednesday 22 year old mark gooch was  convicted of krause's murder she was  found dead in a forest outside flagstaff  arizona in february 2020. krauss was  last seen a month before in her village  near farmington new mexico at the time  gooch was stationed at an air force base  in phoenix but cell phone records show  he drove to krause's village the day she  went missing and his cell phone pinged  near the area where her body was later  found cross's cause of death was  determined to be blunt force trauma and  a gunshot wound to the head on friday  the jury began his deliberation after  hearing closing arguments from both the  defense and prosecution after two days  away from the courthouse the jury  reached a verdict wednesday afternoon  they found gooch guilty of first-degree  murder and kidnapping the judge also  ruled gucci was guilty of the theft of  krause's head covering and underwear  gooch now faces life in prison for his  crimes he is being held without bail  until a sentence hearing on november  24th  back to break down the verdict is  criminal defense attorney julie  randleman and co-host terry austin terry  do you agree with the verdict and was  today the day you thought we'd get a  verdict or did you think the jury would  need more time  brian i definitely agree with this  verdict there's no question that the  evidence was overwhelmingly against the  defendant and for the prosecution i  think that the jury came back in a  reasonable amount of time i don't think  they questioned much of what they heard  i think the only thing they might have  been considering is whether or not this  expert on the gun was right on the  defense side or correct on the  prosecution side but i think all the  rest of the evidence including the cell  phone pings on those towers convinced  the jury that it had to be gooch and he  was the one and the only one who was in  the area who had a motive and who  ultimately killed the victim now julie  did you expect a different verdict than  this and what do you expect income  sentencing  anyone you know who's been following  this case certainly i have was expecting  a very quick conviction i agree with  terry i think the only big issue was the  ballistics experts i thought the  ballistics expert for the prosecution  did a very poor job and i thought the  defense did a fantastic job i mean look  at the end of the day i believe the  sentence is going to be life without  parole um i think this is death penalty  eligible but they chose not to go that  route  and that's what he's going to get and  quite frankly based on the evidence in  this case that seems to be what he  deserves all right we'll see what  happens come sentencing when we come  back the charges that all three  defendants face for the shooting of a  mod arbory and hear from the lead  investigator what he concluded about the  incident in a pre-trial hearing stay  tuned to law and crime daily  [Music]  welcome back we continue to look ahead  to the trial for the death of a mod  arbory where three men stand charged  with his murder  ahmad aubry was a 25 year old unarmed  black man who was shot to death in  february 2020. arbor was out for a jog  in his georgia neighborhood when father  and son travis and gregory mcmichaels  pulled up in their pickup truck and  fired at him the incident was captured  on video by neighbor william bryan three  months later in may 2020 the father and  son were charged with felony murder in  connection to the shooting upon being  arrested the mcmichaels were both denied  bond two weeks later william bryan and  the mcmichael's neighbor was also  charged with felony murder and attempted  false imprisonment the arrest warrants  state that the neighbor tried to quote  confine and detain aubry without legal  authority before the jogger was shot at  a preliminary hearing in june 2020 the  judge ruled that there was enough  probable cause for the murder charges of  all three men this came after hours of  testimony from lead investigator who  gave his opinion on the self-defense  argument  i don't believe it was self-defense by  mr mcmichael i believe it was  self-defense by mr aubry  that's why he took the  once yes i believe mr albert was in  pursuit  and he randomly couldn't run anymore  and it would turn him back to a man with  a shotgun or  or fight with his bare hands against a  man with a shotgun he chose to fight  things to i believe mr aubrey's decision  was to just try to get away when he felt  like he could not escape  he chose to fight  julie malus murder felony aggravated  assault and unlawful imprisonment these  are the charges the big michaels and  brian face how does the prosecution  prove these charges  i think you know as a prosecutor i you  realize that the easiest charge and i i  hate to say easy because nothing's easy  is the felony  murder charges when it comes to the  false imprisonment because that is a way  to get each of those defendants in an  acting in concert capacity without  having to prove intent to kill it's just  the intent to do the um you know  basically you know by following him in  terms of the imprisonment so i think  that as a prosecutor i realize is the  easiest case the malice  murder is much tougher um it's obviously  the easiest it is is for travis  mcmichael he's the person that fired the  weapon he fired three times there's no  question that when someone fires a  weapon the intent is um to do something  pretty serious especially if you're  doing it and aiming it directly at a  person so i think that specific charge  is going to be much more difficult in  terms of proving that with the other two  defendants um and again yeah yeah now  terry the preliminary hearing is  probably what made the federal hate  crime case how much of the trial would  be about those two words traffic  mcmichael said over aubry as he died  those two words are going to be  extremely important brian there's no  question and we heard this from the  hearing that as aubry was dying travis  mcmichael stood over him with his  shotgun in his hand and said racial  slurs and there's also evidence that  there were racial slurs on the social  media of travis mcmichael and frankly  the prosecution is stating that all  three defendants have shown racial slurs  and have been prejudiced so i think it's  a good hate crime case absolutely and  thank you for joining us here on law and  crime daily we'll see you next time as  we discuss justice in america  [Music]  [Music]  [Music]  you
 

